{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-830a54fbf1fc6ce9", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tMeloCodec: Harnessing Melodic Priors for\n2\tHigh-Fidelity Singing Voice Representation\n3\tYizhong Geng∗ , Wenxin Fu∗ , Kecan Mao∗ , Qifei Li∗ , Yingming Gao∗ ,\n4\tRuimin Wang† , Chunfeng Wang† , Hao Li† , Ya Li∗ † , Wei Chen† †\n5\t∗ Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications, Beijing, China\n6\t\n7\tarXiv:2608.03021v1 [cs.SD] 4 Aug 2026\n8\t\n9\t† Li Auto, Beijing, China\n10\t\n11\tAbstract—Neural audio codecs serve as fundamental tokenizers\n12\tfor LLM-based audio generation. While semantic priors are\n13\twidely exploited to enhance linguistic intelligibility, the integration of explicit acoustic priors remains underexplored, limiting\n14\tsynthesis fidelity in frequency-sensitive domains. To address this\n15\tgap, we introduce MeloCodec, a novel framework designed\n16\tto effectively incorporate melodic priors—a critical form of\n17\tacoustic information for singing. To address the optimization\n18\tinstability typically caused by the direct fusion of such explicit\n19\tpriors, we propose a “Tokenize-then-Fuse” paradigm that pretrains a discrete melodic branch to lock in structures before\n20\tfeature fusion. To robustly realize this paradigm, we further\n21\tpropose a robust two-stage training strategy, which prevents\n22\tcodebook collapse and ensures stable convergence. Experiments\n23\tshow that MeloCodec outperforms baselines in singing voice\n24\trepresentation, improving pitch consistency and enabling controllable pitch manipulation with minimal timbre degradation.\n25\tAudio samples are available at https://anonymous.4open.science/\n26\tapi/repo/melocodec demo-60EC/file/demo.html?v=42c197c7.\n27\tIndex Terms—Neural Audio Codec, Singing Voice Representation, Melodic Priors, Residual Vector Quantization\n28\t\n29\tI. I NTRODUCTION\n30\tNeural audio codecs have fundamentally transformed audio\n31\trepresentation learning. Early models focused on high-fidelity\n32\twaveform reconstruction at extremely low bitrate as efficient\n33\talternatives to traditional DSP standards [1], [2]. Following the\n34\tsuccess of LLMs in NLP [3] and CV [4], neural codecs have\n35\tshifted from pure compression to discrete token modeling:\n36\tvector-quantized audio codes bridge continuous waveforms\n37\tand autoregressive generation, turning codecs into foundational “tokenizers” for generative AI. Systems such as VALL-E\n38\t[5], AudioLM [6], and MusicGen [7] rely on these discrete\n39\trepresentations to capture complex acoustic dependencies,\n40\tmaking the codec latent space quality and structure critical\n41\tto generation performance.\n42\tTo satisfy LLM-based generation demands, recent works\n43\tactively integrate semantic priors into the quantization process.\n44\tTypical systems like X-Codec [8], SpeechTokenizer [9], and\n45\tBiCodec [10] fuse SSL vectors, while CosyVoice2 [11] leverages ASR supervision to enhance linguistic alignment. However, prioritizing abstract semantics often neglects the intricate\n46\tacoustic details essential for frequency-sensitive domains like\n47\tsinging voice [12], [13]. Conversely, while approaches like\n48\tFACodec [14] attempt to model acoustics via implicit latent\n49\t† Corresponding authors\n50\t\n51\tdisentanglement, they lack rigid structural guarantees, often\n52\trelying on unstable multi-objective constraints that are prone\n53\tto information leakage.\n54\tTo overcome the limitations of semantic-only approaches\n55\tand implicit disentanglement, we propose MeloCodec, a novel\n56\tneural audio codec that explicitly integrates melodic priors into\n57\tthe quantization process. While recent emerging works have\n58\tbegun to explore explicit priors like Chromagrams for synthesis [15]–[17], simply concatenating them within a codec’s\n59\tlatent space introduces a critical challenge: Shortcut Learning\n60\t[18]. Since the acoustic branch (processing raw waveforms)\n61\tinherently contains superset information of the melody branch,\n62\tthe decoder tends to ignore the explicit melodic features,\n63\trendering the fusion ineffective [19], [20]. Furthermore, the\n64\tdirect fusion of these deterministic priors with flexible neural\n65\tlatents causes severe optimization instability due to gradient\n66\tconflicts [18], [19], [21]–[23].\n67\tTo address these challenges, we introduce MeloCodec,\n68\ta novel framework built upon a “Tokenize-then-Fuse”\n69\tparadigm. By enforcing an Information Bottleneck, this\n70\tparadigm tokenizes melodic priors into quantized tokens to\n71\tlock in structures before feature fusion, thereby reducing\n72\ttimbral leakage and encouraging the acoustic stream to rely on\n73\tthe melodic branch. To robustly realize this, we further propose\n74\ta two-stage training strategy that resolves feature conflicts,\n75\tprevents codebook collapse, and ensures stable convergence\n76\twhere direct fusion fails. Ultimately, MeloCodec establishes a\n77\tscalable blueprint for bridging interpretable physical models\n78\twith generative neural networks, proving that imposing discrete bottlenecks is essential for structurally grounded LLMbased audio generation.\n79\tOur principal contributions are summarized as follows:\n80\tWe propose MeloCodec, a novel codec framework that\n81\tintegrates explicit melodic priors into the quantization\n82\tloop. Unlike previous conditioning methods, we embed\n83\tmelodic structures directly into the discrete latent space,\n84\testablishing a unified and disentangled token interface for\n85\tLLM-based generation.\n86\t• We introduce a “Tokenize-then-Fuse” paradigm to incorporate explicit melodic priors via an Information Bottleneck, and devise a robust two-stage training strategy\n87\tto address shortcut learning and optimization instability,\n88\tensuring stable convergence.\n89\t•\n90\t\n91\t\fTokenize Branch\n92\t\n93\tChromagram\n94\tExtraction\n95\t\n96\tMelody\n97\tChromagram �\n98\t\n99\t�\n100\t\n101\t�\n102\t\n103\t��\n104\t\n105\t�\n106\tMelody Feature\n107\tAcoustic Feature\n108\tProjected Feature\n109\t\n110\t�\n111\t\n112\t��\n113\t\n114\tK\n115\t\n116\tRVQ\n117\t\n118\tProjection\n119\t\n120\tC\n121\t\n122\t��\n123\t\n124\tConcat\n125\tAcoustic\n126\tReconstruction\n127\t\n128\t��\n129\t\n130\tReconstruction\n131\tChromagram �\n132\t\n133\tProjection\n134\t\n135\tRVQ\n136\t\n137\t��\n138\t\n139\tMelody\n140\tDecoder\n141\t\n142\tProjection\n143\t\n144\tAudio Wav W\n145\t\n146\tEncoder\n147\t\n148\tProjection\n149\t\n150\tAcoustic\n151\t\n152\tChromagram\n153\tReconstruction �\n154\t\n155\tEncoder\n156\t\n157\t��\n158\t\n159\tFuse Branch\n160\t\n161\t�\n162\t\n163\t��\n164\t\n165\tQuantized Melody\n166\tQuantized Acoustic\n167\tQuantized Predicted\n168\t\n169\tAcoustic\n170\t\n171\t��\n172\t\n173\t��\n174\t\n175\tDecoder\n176\t\n177\tReconstruction\n178\tAudio Wav �\n179\tQuantized Unified\n180\t\n181\tSTAGE 1：Melody Codec Training\n182\tSTAGE 2：Joint Codec Training\n183\t\n184\tFig. 1. The architecture of MeloCodec implementing the “Tokenize-then-Fuse” paradigm. The framework consists of a Tokenize Branch (Green) for\n185\tdiscrete structural modeling and a Fuse Branch (Blue) for acoustic reconstruction. The switch K governs the data flow across the robust two-stage training\n186\tstrategy, where dashed boxes explicitly delimit the scope of active gradient updates: (1) In Stage 1, K routes the quantized melody Mq directly to the decoder\n187\tcq ), allowing gradients to update the Fuse Branch and fine-tune the\n188\tto pre-train the bottleneck; (2) In Stage 2, K switches to the predicted auxiliary path (M\n189\tMelody Decoder, while the pre-trained Melody Encoder and RVQ remains frozen.\n190\t\n191\t•\n192\t\n193\tWe demonstrate State-of-the-Art Performance through\n194\tevaluations on singing voice datasets, showing that MeloCodec significantly outperforms baselines in pitch consistency. Furthermore, we verify that our model enables\n195\tcontrollable pitch manipulation with minimal timbre\n196\tdegradation, confirming the effectiveness of our disentangled representation.\n197\tII. M ETHOD\n198\t\n199\ting structural progressions from spectral details, this branch\n200\tcreates a disentangled melodic reference for the system.\n201\tGiven the input audio waveform x ∈ RT , we first extract\n202\tthe chromagram C ∈ RF ×N (where F = 12 for pitch classes\n203\tand N for time frames) using Short-Time Fourier Transform\n204\t(STFT) followed by pitch-class projection: C = Φ(x), where\n205\tΦ(·) acts as a physical filter to discard envelope information.\n206\tThe chromagram is then encoded by a convolutional melody\n207\tencoder Emel into continuous latent features M:\n208\t\n209\tA. Overview of Architecture\n210\tAs shown in Fig. 1, MeloCodec decouples structural modeling from texture synthesis via a “Tokenize-then-Fuse”\n211\tparadigm. Given waveform x, the framework comprises two\n212\tinteracting streams: 1) The Tokenize Branch extracts melodic\n213\tpriors (chromagrams) and quantizes them into tokens Mq via\n214\tRVQ, creating an information bottleneck to filter timbral noise;\n215\t2) The Fuse Branch encodes acoustic features A and integrates the pre-calculated Mq for high-fidelity reconstruction.\n216\tTo address the severe optimization instability of direct fusion,\n217\twe employ a two-stage training strategy.\n218\tB. Tokenize Branch: Discrete Melodic Modeling\n219\tThe Tokenize Branch leverages chromagrams to capture a\n220\tspeaker-invariant ”melodic priors” [24]. By explicitly isolat-\n221\t\n222\tM = Emel (C),\n223\t\n224\tM ∈ Rd×N\n225\t\n226\t′\n227\t\n228\t(1)\n229\t\n230\twhere d is the hidden channel dimension and N ′ is the\n231\tdownsampled temporal resolution.\n232\tTo discretize M into compact tokens, we apply RVQ with\n233\tNq levels and codebook Z, accumulating residuals for a\n234\tcoarse-to-fine representation:\n235\t\n236\tMq =\n237\t\n238\tNq\n239\tX\n240\tk=1\n241\t\n242\t\n243\tzk ,\n244\t\n245\twhere zk = Quantizek M −\n246\t\n247\tk−1\n248\tX\n249\t\n250\t\n251\tzj  (2)\n252\t\n253\tj=1\n254\t\n255\tThis information bottleneck filters redundant noise, enforcing the latent space to capture essential structural semanb =\n256\ttics. A symmetric melody decoder Dmel reconstructs C\n257\t\n258\t\fDmel (Mq ). We pre-train this branch independently using the\n259\tVQ-VAE objective:\n260\tb 2 + ∥sg[Emel (C)] − Mq ∥2\n261\tLstage1 = ∥C − C∥\n262\t2\n263\t2\n264\t\n265\t(3)\n266\t\n267\twhere sg[·] denotes the stop-gradient operator. This pretraining explicitly locks in structures, establishing a robust\n268\tmelodic ”skeleton” that prevents codebook collapse in later\n269\tjoint optimization.\n270\tC. Fuse Branch: Heterogeneous Feature Fusion\n271\tThe Fuse Branch integrates discrete priors (Mq ) with\n272\tacoustic features. Crucially, fusing quantized tokens imposes\n273\tan information bottleneck, preventing the shortcut learning and\n274\toptimization instability inherent in continuous feature fusion.\n275\tThe raw waveform x is processed by a convolutional\n276\tacoustic encoder Eac to yield dense features A. Concurrently,\n277\tthe quantized melody tokens Mq from the Tokenize Branch\n278\tare aligned to the acoustic space via a linear projection ψ(·):\n279\tA = Eac (x),\n280\t\n281\tMp = ψ(Mq )\n282\t\n283\t(4)\n284\t\n285\td×N ′\n286\t\n287\twhere A, Mp ∈ R\n288\t, with d as the hidden channel\n289\tdimension and N ′ as the downsampled temporal resolution\n290\tmatching the melodic stream. Fusing Mq rather than continuous M imposes a discrete information bottleneck, preventing\n291\tshortcut learning where the decoder bypasses structural priors\n292\tby relying on residual acoustic details.\n293\tThe fused representation U is obtained via concatenation\n294\tand then discretized into unified tokens Uq via a RVQ:\n295\tU = Concat(A, Mp )\n296\t\n297\t(5)\n298\t\n299\tTo enforce melodic integrity in Uq , an auxiliary projection\n300\tc q = ϕ(Uq ), which reconstructs the\n301\thead ϕ(·) predicts M\n302\tchromagram through the melody decoder. The auxiliary supervision loss is defined as:\n303\tc q )∥2\n304\tLaux = ∥C − Dmel (M\n305\t2\n306\t\n307\t(6)\n308\t\n309\tThis supervision backpropagates gradients from the pretrained decoder, embedding acoustic information into the\n310\tunified representation without dominating acoustic textures.\n311\tD. Training Strategy: Robust Two-Stage Optimization for\n312\t“Tokenize-then-Fuse”\n313\tTo robustly realize the “Tokenize-then-Fuse” paradigm\n314\tand resolve the severe optimization instability arising from\n315\tdirect fusion between deterministic priors and flexible neural\n316\tlatents, we employ a robust two-stage training strategy. This\n317\tstrategy first isolates the Tokenize Branch to lock in structures\n318\tfor stability, and then jointly optimizes the Fuse Branch with\n319\tanchored discrete priors, ensuring stable convergence where\n320\tdirect fusion fails.\n321\tIn Stage 1 (Melody Codec Pre-training), only the Tokenize\n322\tBranch is active: the melody encoder Emel , RVQ, and decoder\n323\tDmel are fully trained using Lstage1 (Eq. 3) to build a compact\n324\tcodebook of pitch transitions. This phase effectively locks in\n325\tstructures, keeping them free from acoustic interference.\n326\t\n327\tIn Stage 2 (Joint Codec Training), we employ a partial\n328\tfreezing strategy: while Emel and its RVQ remain frozen to\n329\tanchor discrete priors, the melody decoder Dmel is fine-tuned.\n330\tEmpirically, we observe that a frozen decoder imposes overly\n331\trigid manifold constraints, causing optimization conflicts. Unfreezing Dmel allows it to accommodate distribution shifts in\n332\tthe unified tokens. This strategy effectively prevents codebook\n333\tcollapse and guarantees the stable convergence of the joint\n334\toptimization. The Fuse Branch (Acoustic Stream) is optimized\n335\tend-to-end. Specifically, Lrec comprises L1 waveform loss and\n336\tmulti-scale STFT loss. We employ a MSD for Ladv , trained\n337\tvia a hinge loss objective with feature matching regularization.\n338\tThe total objective is:\n339\tb) + λadv Ladv + λaux Laux\n340\tLstage2 = Lrec (x, x\n341\t\n342\t(7)\n343\t\n344\tThis setup ensures robust supervision flow with minimal\n345\ttimbre degradation.\n346\tIII. E XPERIMENTS\n347\tA. Experimental Setup\n348\tDatasets and Baselines. Models were trained on an internal\n349\tcorpus of 5,500 hours of Mandarin singing voice (44.1kHz)\n350\tand evaluated on the held-out Opencpop dataset [27]. To\n351\tisolate architectural contributions, we compared MeloCodec\n352\tagainst EnCodec [2], DAC [25], and X-Codec [8], all finetuned on our internal data. We also include MuCodec [26]\n353\t(official weights) as a zero-shot reference for low-bitrate\n354\tmusic, as its training code is unavailable.\n355\tImplementation Details. We use a codebook size of 1024.\n356\tPerformance is evaluated under high bitrate (Nq = 8, ≈6.0\n357\tkbps) and low bitrate (Nq = 2, ≈1.5 kbps) settings. Crucially,\n358\tmelodic tokens are priors only; reported bitrates are calculated exclusively on transmitted unified tokens Uq . Training\n359\temployed AdamW and standard EMA codebook updates on\n360\tNVIDIA H800 GPUs. Inference uses a sliding window (50%\n361\toverlap) to ensure continuity.\n362\tMetrics. Audio quality is measured via ViSQOL [28]\n363\tand STOI [29]; pitch accuracy via F0-RMSE and ChromaSIM; and timbre via SPK-SIM (ResNet-34) [30]. Subjective\n364\tquality was assessed via a MUSHRA test [31] (15 experts,\n365\t30 samples). Controllability is quantified by Target F0-Corr\n366\tunder pitch shifting (±2, ±4 semitones).\n367\tB. Reconstruction Performance\n368\tObjective Evaluation. Table I shows that in the high bitrate\n369\tsetting, MeloCodec outperforms the strong baseline DAC [25],\n370\tconfirming that integrating melodic priors enhances fidelity\n371\twithout interference.\n372\tThe advantages are most typical in the low bitrate setting.\n373\tAs bandwidth tightens (Nq reduced to 2), traditional codecs\n374\tdegrade sharply in pitch consistency (e.g., Encodec’s F0RMSE nearly triples), suggesting standard RVQ neglects essential acoustic structural information. In contrast, MeloCodec\n375\tremains robust (F0-RMSE 1.64). Furthermore, unlike MuCodec [26], which preserves melody but suffers in general\n376\tquality (ViSQOL 2.62) due to low frame rates, MeloCodec\n377\t\n378\t\fTABLE I\n379\tO BJECTIVE PERFORMANCE COMPARISON WITH STATE - OF - THE - ART BASELINES ON THE O PEN C POP TEST SET. T O ENSURE FAIR COMPARISON , WE\n380\tEVALUATE MODELS UNDER TWO DISTINCT BITRATE SETTINGS : HIGH BITRATE (≈ 6.0 KBPS ) AND LOW BITRATE (≈ 1.5 KBPS ). T HE RESULTS\n381\tHIGHLIGHT M ELO C ODEC ’ S ROBUSTNESS IN PRESERVING MELODIC STRUCTURE EVEN UNDER STRICTLY CONSTRAINED BANDWIDTHS .\n382\t\n383\tBitrate\n384\t\n385\tModel\n386\t\n387\t(kbps)\n388\t\n389\tArchitecture\n390\tCodebook\n391\tSize\n392\t\n393\tNq\n394\t\n395\tGeneral Audio Quality\n396\t\n397\tToken Rate\n398\t(Hz)\n399\t\n400\tViSQOL ↑\n401\t\n402\tSTOI ↑\n403\t\n404\tMelody & Pitch\n405\t\n406\tTimbre\n407\t\n408\tF0-RMSE ↓\n409\t\n410\tChroma-Sim ↑\n411\t\n412\tSPK-SIM ↑\n413\t\n414\t1.92\n415\t1.23\n416\t1.35\n417\t0.96\n418\t\n419\t0.93\n420\t0.96\n421\t0.95\n422\t0.97\n423\t\n424\t0.97\n425\t0.98\n426\t0.96\n427\t0.99\n428\t\n429\t4.87\n430\t4.12\n431\t4.68\n432\t2.68\n433\t1.64\n434\t\n435\t0.81\n436\t0.84\n437\t0.83\n438\t0.92\n439\t0.95\n440\t\n441\t0.88\n442\t0.91\n443\t0.87\n444\t0.80\n445\t0.94\n446\t\n447\thigh bitrate Setting (≈6.0 kbps)\n448\tEncodec [2]\n449\tDAC [25]\n450\tX-Codec [8]\n451\tMeloCodec (Ours)\n452\t\n453\t6.00\n454\t6.00\n455\t6.00\n456\t6.00\n457\t\n458\t1024\n459\t1024\n460\t1024\n461\t1024\n462\t\n463\t8\n464\t8\n465\t8\n466\t8\n467\t\n468\t75\n469\t75\n470\t75\n471\t75\n472\t\n473\t3.92\n474\t3.97\n475\t3.34\n476\t4.08\n477\t\n478\t0.82\n479\t0.83\n480\t0.81\n481\t0.85\n482\t\n483\tlow bitrate Setting (≈1.5 kbps)\n484\tEncodec [2]\n485\tDAC [25]\n486\tX-Codec [8]\n487\tMuCodec [26]\n488\tMeloCodec (Ours)\n489\t\n490\t100\n491\t\n492\t1.50\n493\t1.50\n494\t1.50\n495\t1.33\n496\t1.50\n497\t\n498\t1024\n499\t1024\n500\t1024\n501\t10000\n502\t1024\n503\t\n504\t2\n505\t2\n506\t2\n507\t4\n508\t2\n509\t\n510\t75\n511\t75\n512\t75\n513\t25\n514\t75\n515\t\n516\tSubjective Quality Evaluation (MUSHRA)\n517\t\n518\t90\n519\t\n520\tMUSHRA Score\n521\t\n522\t80\n523\t70\n524\t60\n525\tOriginal Music\n526\tEncodec\n527\tX-Codec\n528\tMuCodec\n529\tDAC\n530\tMeloCodec (Ours)\n531\t\n532\t50\n533\t40\n534\t30\n535\t\n536\tLow Bitrate\n537\t( 1.5 kbps)\n538\t\n539\tBitrate (kbps)\n540\t\n541\tHigh Bitrate\n542\t( 6.0 kbps)\n543\t\n544\tFig. 2. Subjective MUSHRA evaluation results comparing MeloCodec\n545\twith baselines across varying bitrates. The shaded areas represent the 95%\n546\tconfidence intervals.\n547\t\n548\teffectively bridges this gap, delivering superior melodic integrity with minimal timbre degradation.\n549\tSubjective Evaluation. To corroborate the objective metrics, we conducted a MUSHRA (Multi-Stimulus Test with\n550\tHidden Reference and Anchor) listening test to evaluate perceptual quality. As illustrated in Figure 2, the subjective results\n551\talign consistently with the objective data. At high bitrate, MeloCodec achieves the highest mean score, closely approaching\n552\tthe reference recording. Crucially, in the low-bitrate scenario,\n553\tMeloCodec maintains robust performance superiority. While\n554\tlisteners reported noticeable artifacts and pitch instability in\n555\tsamples generated by baselines such as X-Codec and Encodec,\n556\tMeloCodec retained clear melodic progressions and natural\n557\ttimbre. This underscores the effectiveness of our auxiliary\n558\tmelody supervision, which acts as a stable melodic prior to\n559\tprevent codebook collapse under extreme compression rates.\n560\t\n561\t2.81\n562\t3.05\n563\t2.74\n564\t2.62\n565\t3.38\n566\t\n567\t0.68\n568\t0.71\n569\t0.66\n570\t0.57\n571\t0.78\n572\t\n573\tC. Melodic Integrity and Pitch Analysis\n574\tTo validate the model’s capability in preserving and manipulating musical structures, we conduct a focused analysis on\n575\tboth reconstruction stability and latent disentanglement.\n576\tReconstruction Stability. As detailed in Table I, MeloCodec consistently surpasses baselines in pitch accuracy.\n577\tThe advantage is most critical in the low bitrate setting\n578\t(≈1.5 kbps). MeloCodec remains robust (F0-RMSE 1.64),\n579\tacting as a structural anchor. This is visually corroborated in\n580\tFigure 3, where our model captures subtle vibratos without\n581\tthe frequency jitter and octave errors observed in baseline\n582\treconstructions.\n583\tDisentanglement & Controllability. We evaluate controllability by shifting melodic priors (±2/4 semitones) while keeping acoustic features fixed. As shown in Table III, MeloCodec\n584\tmaintains robust contour correlation (0.70–0.72), confirming\n585\teffective melodic drive. However, the elevated RMSE (3.85–\n586\t4.50) reveals a trade-off: while the contour shifts, absolute\n587\tpitch precision remains partially constrained by the fixed\n588\tacoustic branch. In contrast, Direct Fusion fails completely\n589\t(RMSE 6.80), confirming its vulnerability to shortcut learning.\n590\tD. Ablation Study: Efficacy of Training Strategies\n591\tTo validate the advantage of our proposed “Tokenize-thenFuse” paradigm, we conducted ablation studies comparing\n592\tMeloCodec against three variants: (1) Acoustic-only, a standard single-stream codec similar to DAC; (2) + Auxiliary\n593\tLoss, single-stream codec trained with additional melody\n594\treconstruction supervision; and (3) Direct Fusion, a dualstream architecture trained end-to-end without the two-stage\n595\ttraining strategy. The results are summarized in Table II.\n596\tBenefit of Melodic Supervision. Comparing the first two\n597\trows, adding an auxiliary melody loss yields a noticeable\n598\timprovement in pitch accuracy, reducing F0-RMSE from 4.12\n599\tto 2.10 in the low-bitrate setting. This confirms that explicit\n600\tsupervisory signals can guide the encoder to retain more\n601\t\n602\t\fTABLE II\n603\tA BLATION STUDY OF M ELO C ODEC TRAINING STRATEGIES ON THE O PEN C POP TEST SET. W E COMPARE DIFFERENT FUSION AND SUPERVISION\n604\tMETHODS USING C HROMAGRAM FEATURES . T HE RESULTS HIGHLIGHT THAT THE PROPOSED “T OKENIZE - THEN -F USE ” PARADIGM (O URS ) IS ESSENTIAL\n605\tFOR STABILITY AND PERFORMANCE , ESPECIALLY AT LOW BITRATE .\n606\t\n607\tBitrate\n608\t\n609\tMethod\n610\t\n611\tArchitecture\n612\t\n613\t(kbps)\n614\t\n615\tCodebook\n616\tSize\n617\t\n618\tNq\n619\t\n620\tAcoustic-only\n621\t+ Auxiliary Loss\n622\tDirect Fusion\n623\tOurs (Tokenize-then-Fuse)\n624\t\n625\t6.00\n626\t6.00\n627\t6.00\n628\t6.00\n629\t\n630\t1024\n631\t1024\n632\t1024\n633\t1024\n634\t\n635\t8\n636\t8\n637\t8\n638\t8\n639\t\n640\tAcoustic-only\n641\t+ Auxiliary Loss\n642\tDirect Fusion\n643\tOurs (Tokenize-then-Fuse)\n644\t\n645\t1.50\n646\t1.50\n647\t1.50\n648\t1.50\n649\t\n650\t1024\n651\t1024\n652\t1024\n653\t1024\n654\t\n655\t2\n656\t2\n657\t2\n658\t2\n659\t\n660\tGeneral Audio Quality\n661\t\n662\tToken Rate\n663\t(Hz)\n664\t\n665\tViSQOL ↑\n666\t\n667\tMelody & Pitch\n668\t\n669\tTimbre\n670\t\n671\tSTOI ↑\n672\t\n673\tF0-RMSE ↓\n674\t\n675\tChroma-Sim ↑\n676\t\n677\tSPK-SIM ↑\n678\t\n679\t0.83\n680\t0.84\n681\t0.79\n682\t0.85\n683\t\n684\t1.23\n685\t1.15\n686\t2.30\n687\t0.96\n688\t\n689\t0.96\n690\t0.96\n691\t0.93\n692\t0.97\n693\t\n694\t0.98\n695\t0.98\n696\t0.96\n697\t0.99\n698\t\n699\t0.71\n700\t0.75\n701\t0.58\n702\t0.78\n703\t\n704\t4.12\n705\t2.10\n706\t5.95\n707\t1.64\n708\t\n709\t0.84\n710\t0.91\n711\t0.81\n712\t0.95\n713\t\n714\t0.91\n715\t0.93\n716\t0.85\n717\t0.94\n718\t\n719\thigh bitrate Setting (≈6.0 kbps)\n720\t75\n721\t75\n722\t75\n723\t75\n724\t\n725\t3.97\n726\t3.98\n727\t3.52\n728\t4.08\n729\t\n730\tlow bitrate Setting (≈1.5 kbps)\n731\t75\n732\t75\n733\t75\n734\t75\n735\t\n736\t3.05\n737\t3.25\n738\t2.34\n739\t3.38\n740\t\n741\tTABLE III\n742\tE VALUATION OF DISENTANGLEMENT VIA P ITCH S HIFTING . N OTE THAT\n743\tWHILE ABSOLUTE PITCH PRECISION (RMSE) IS LIMITED BY ACOUSTIC\n744\tCONSTRAINTS , M ELO C ODEC MAINTAINS SUPERIOR MELODIC CONTOUR\n745\t(C ORR ) COMPARED TO D IRECT F USION .\n746\tMethod\n747\t\n748\tPitch Control\n749\t\n750\tTimbre Stability\n751\t\n752\tShift\n753\t(Semi.)\n754\t\n755\tTarget F0-Corr ↑\n756\t\n757\tTarget F0-RMSE ↓\n758\t\n759\tSPK-SIM ↑\n760\t\n761\tDegrade % ↓\n762\t\n763\tDirect Fusion\n764\t\n765\t±2\n766\t±4\n767\t\n768\t0.40\n769\t0.27\n770\t\n771\t4.10\n772\t6.80\n773\t\n774\t0.88\n775\t0.82\n776\t\n777\t7.6%\n778\t14.0%\n779\t\n780\tMeloCodec\n781\t\n782\t±2\n783\t±4\n784\t\n785\t0.72\n786\t0.70\n787\t\n788\t3.85\n789\t4.50\n790\t\n791\t0.93\n792\t0.91\n793\t\n794\t1.6%\n795\t3.9%\n796\t\n797\tpitch information. However, the gain in general audio quality\n798\t(ViSQOL) is marginal, suggesting that soft constraints via loss\n799\tfunctions alone are insufficient to fundamentally restructure\n800\tthe latent space.\n801\tInstability of Direct Fusion. We analyze the Direct Fusion\n802\tstrategy, representing the standard ”One-stage” paradigm. To\n803\tensure fairness, this baseline employs learnable projection\n804\tlayers (MLP) and normalization to align heterogeneous modalities, avoiding simple concatenation. Despite these safeguards,\n805\tthe method exhibits catastrophic degradation (F0-RMSE worsens to 5.95), confirming severe optimization instability.\n806\tDiagnostic analysis of training dynamics reveals the root\n807\tcause as modality collapse driven by feature competition.\n808\tWe observed two failure modes: 1) Codebook Collapse:\n809\tCodebook utilization dropped to < 5% early in training,\n810\tindicating the discrete bottleneck was bypassed. 2) Optimization Divergence: Crucially, the auxiliary chroma validation\n811\tloss diverged continuously while training loss decreased. This\n812\tconfirms shortcut learning: dense acoustic gradients dominate\n813\toptimization, causing the decoder to overfit acoustic textures\n814\trather than attending to rigid melodic priors. This verifies that\n815\tstandard architectural tuning (e.g., scaling or gating) is insufficient; rather, the proposed “Tokenize-then-Fuse” paradigm is\n816\tstructurally necessary to enforce effective melodic encoding.\n817\tEffectiveness of “Tokenize-then-Fuse”. In contrast, our\n818\tstrategy achieves the best overall performance. By decoupling\n819\t\n820\tFig. 3. Visual comparison of reconstructed pitch contours (F0). The upper\n821\tpanel shows F0 trajectories with the shaded area indicating MeloCodec’s\n822\tdeviation. The lower panel presents error metrics RMSE and MAE.\n823\t\n824\ttraining into two stages and fusing quantized melody tokens\n825\t(Mq ), we effectively resolve severe optimization instability.\n826\tThe pre-trained Melodic Stream explicitly locks in structures,\n827\tallowing the Acoustic Stream to focus on texture synthesis.\n828\tThis is evident at low bitrate, where our method ensures stable\n829\tconvergence (F0-RMSE 1.64) compared to the collapse in\n830\tDirect Fusion.\n831\tIV. C ONCLUSION\n832\tIn this paper, we presented MeloCodec, a dual-stream\n833\tneural audio codec that explicitly integrates melodic priors\n834\tto achieve high-fidelity singing voice representation. By addressing the severe optimization instability inherent in the\n835\tdirect fusion of features, our proposed “Tokenize-then-Fuse”\n836\tparadigm effectively locks in structures via a pre-trained\n837\tdiscrete branch, ensuring stable convergence and preventing\n838\tcodebook collapse. Results demonstrate that this strategy\n839\tsignificantly outperforms baselines in pitch consistency with\n840\tminimal timbre degradation. Crucially, our framework establishes a scalable blueprint for bridging interpretable physical\n841\t\n842\t\fmodels with generative neural networks, proving that imposing\n843\tdiscrete bottlenecks on deterministic priors is essential for\n844\trobust feature integration. This opens new avenues for future\n845\tresearch to extend the disentangled interface by incorporating\n846\tadditional explicit acoustic priors, such as rhythmic patterns\n847\tand dynamic contours, paving the way for fully controllable\n848\tand structurally grounded LLM-based audio generation.\n849\tV. ACKNOWLEDGMENT\n850\tThe work was supported by the National Natural Science\n851\tFoundation of China (NSFC) (No. 62271083), and the Key\n852\tProject of the National Language Commission (No. ZDI14581).\n853\tR EFERENCES\n854\t[1] Neil Zeghidour, Alejandro Luebs, Ahmed Omran, Jan Skoglund, and\n855\tMarco Tagliasacchi, “Soundstream: An end-to-end neural audio codec,”\n856\tIEEE/ACM Transactions on Audio, Speech, and Language Processing,\n857\tvol. 30, pp. 495–507, 2021.\n858\t[2] Alexandre Défossez, Jade Copet, Gabriel Synnaeve, and Yossi\n859\tAdi, “High fidelity neural audio compression,” arXiv preprint\n860\tarXiv:2210.13438, 2022.\n861\t[3] Tom Brown, Benjamin Mann, Nick Ryder, Melanie Subbiah, Jared D\n862\tKaplan, Prafulla Dhariwal, Arvind Neelakantan, Pranav Shyam, Girish\n863\tSastry, Amanda Askell, et al., “Language models are few-shot learners,”\n864\tAdvances in neural information processing systems, vol. 33, pp. 1877–\n865\t1901, 2020.\n866\t[4] Aditya Ramesh, Mikhail Pavlov, Gabriel Goh, Scott Gray, Chelsea Voss,\n867\tAlec Radford, Mark Chen, and Ilya Sutskever, “Zero-shot text-to-image\n868\tgeneration,” in International conference on machine learning. Pmlr,\n869\t2021, pp. 8821–8831.\n870\t[5] Chengyi Wang, Sanyuan Chen, Yu Wu, Ziqiang Zhang, Long Zhou,\n871\tShujie Liu, Zhuo Chen, Yanqing Liu, Huaming Wang, Jinyu Li, et al.,\n872\t“Neural codec language models are zero-shot text to speech synthesizers,” arXiv preprint ar