{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b921e43324cff7f4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "=== 2210.13438 ===\n# High Fidelity Neural Audio Compression\narXiv: 2210.13438\nAuthors: Alexandre Défossez, Jade Copet, Gabriel Synnaeve, Yossi Adi\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of a state-of-the-art real-time, high-fidelity neural audio codec using a streaming encoder-decoder architecture with quantized latent space.\n  2. Simplification and acceleration of training by using a single multiscale spectrogram adversary that efficiently reduces artifacts and produces high-quality samples.\n  3. Introduction of a novel loss balancer mechanism that stabilizes training by defining loss weights as the fraction of the overall gradient they represent, decoupling them from the natural scale of the loss.\n  4. Demonstration that lightweight Transformer models can further compress the representation by up to 40% while remaining faster than real time.\n  5. Superiority of the proposed approach over baseline methods across all evaluated settings, including both 24 kHz monophonic and 48 kHz stereophonic audio.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学\n=== 2308.16692 ===\n# SpeechTokenizer: Unified Speech Tokenizer for Speech Large Language Models\narXiv: 2308.16692\nAuthors: Xin Zhang, Dong Zhang, Shimin Li, Yaqian Zhou, Xipeng Qiu\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose SpeechTokenizer, a unified speech tokenizer for speech language models that hierarchically disentangles different aspects of speech information across RVQ layers to unify semantic and acoustic tokens.\n  2. We establish SLMTokBench, the first benchmark to assess the suitability of speech tokens for constructing speech language models.\n  3. We construct a unified speech language model based on SpeechTokenizer that outperforms VALL-E on zero-shot text-to-speech tasks.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, 压缩/语义声学-混合\n=== 2403.03100 ===\n# NaturalSpeech 3: Zero-Shot Speech Synthesis with Factorized Codec and Diffusion Models\narXiv: 2403.03100\nAuthors: Zeqian Ju, Yuancheng Wang, Kai Shen, Xu Tan, Detai Xin, Dongchao Yang, Yanqing Liu, Yichong Leng, Kaitao Song, Siliang Tang, Zhizheng Wu, Tao Qin, Xiang-Yang Li, Wei Ye, Shikun Zhang, Jiang Bian, Lei He, Jinyu Li, Sheng Zhao\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A novel neural speech codec with factorized vector quantization is proposed to disentangle speech waveforms into distinct subspaces of content, prosody, timbre, and acoustic details.\n  2. A factorized diffusion model is introduced to generate speech attributes in each disentangled subspace following their corresponding prompts in a divide-and-conquer manner.\n  3. The system achieves state-of-the-art performance and human-level naturalness on the multi-speaker LibriSpeech dataset for zero-shot text-to-speech synthesis.\n  4. The factorization design enables flexible speech attribute manipulation by allowing different prompts to control different attributes independently.\n  5. The effectiveness of the system is further improved by scaling the model to 1 billion parameters and the training data to 200K hours.\n\nTags: TTS, 压缩, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion, TTS/控制-参考语音, 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, 压缩/语义声学-混合\n=== 2409.13216 ===\nPaper 2409.13216 not found\n=== 2509.19883 ===\nPaper 2509.19883 not found\n=== 2509.14052 ===\nPaper 2509.14052 not found\n=== 2508.16332 ===\n# Vevo2: A Unified and Controllable Framework for Speech and Singing Voice Generation\narXiv: 2508.16332\nAuthors: Xueyao Zhang, Junan Zhang, Yuancheng Wang, Chaoren Wang, Yuanzhe Chen, Dongya Jia, Zhuo Chen, Zhizheng Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose two audio tokenizers: a unified music-notation-free prosody tokenizer capable of encoding prosody and melody from speech, singing voice, and even instrumental sounds, and a unified timbre-disentangled content-style tokenizer that models linguistic content, prosody, and style for both speech and singing voice.\n  2. We introduce Vevo2, a unified and controllable framework for both speech and singing voice generation that applies versatile voice synthesis, conversion, and editing tasks by flexibly combining text, prosodic source, and style and timbre references during inference.\n  3. We propose a multi-objective post-training task for controllable voice generation that optimizes both intelligibility and prosody similarity rewards with a GRPO-based algorithm to achieve greater stability and improved overall performance.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-参考语音, TTS/控制-风格标签, TTS/功能-RL优化, TTS/功能-多说话人", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c4ababf3-da78-4d99-91c1-4b1a994675f0", "uuid": "c3319ec7-11aa-4f9f-ae67-4eab0da998cd", "timestamp": "2026-08-05T07:26:49.602Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "=== 2210.13438 ===\n# High Fidelity Neural Audio Compression\narXiv: 2210.13438\nAuthors: Alexandre Défossez, Jade Copet, Gabriel Synnaeve, Yossi Adi\nYear: 2022 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of a state-of-the-art real-time, high-fidelity neural audio codec using a streaming encoder-decoder architecture with quantized latent space.\n  2. Simplification and acceleration of training by using a single multiscale spectrogram adversary that efficiently reduces artifacts and produces high-quality samples.\n  3. Introduction of a novel loss balancer mechanism that stabilizes training by defining loss weights as the fraction of the overall gradient they represent, decoupling them from the natural scale of the loss.\n  4. Demonstration that lightweight Transformer models can further compress the representation by up to 40% while remaining faster than real time.\n  5. Superiority of the proposed approach over baseline methods across all evaluated settings, including both 24 kHz monophonic and 48 kHz stereophonic audio.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学\n=== 2308.16692 ===\n# SpeechTokenizer: Unified Speech Tokenizer for Speech Large Language Models\narXiv: 2308.16692\nAuthors: Xin Zhang, Dong Zhang, Shimin Li, Yaqian Zhou, Xipeng Qiu\nYear: 2023 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose SpeechTokenizer, a unified speech tokenizer for speech language models that hierarchically disentangles different aspects of speech information across RVQ layers to unify semantic and acoustic tokens.\n  2. We establish SLMTokBench, the first benchmark to assess the suitability of speech tokens for constructing speech language models.\n  3. We construct a unified speech language model based on SpeechTokenizer that outperforms VALL-E on zero-shot text-to-speech tasks.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-RVQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, 压缩/语义声学-混合\n=== 2403.03100 ===\n# NaturalSpeech 3: Zero-Shot Speech Synthesis with Factorized Codec and Diffusion Models\narXiv: 2403.03100\nAuthors: Zeqian Ju, Yuancheng Wang, Kai Shen, Xu Tan, Detai Xin, Dongchao Yang, Yanqing Liu, Yichong Leng, Kaitao Song, Siliang Tang, Zhizheng Wu, Tao Qin, Xiang-Yang Li, Wei Ye, Shikun Zhang, Jiang Bian, Lei He, Jinyu Li, Sheng Zhao\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. A novel neural speech codec with factorized vector quantization is proposed to disentangle speech waveforms into distinct subspaces of content, prosody, timbre, and acoustic details.\n  2. A factorized diffusion model is introduced to generate speech attributes in each disentangled subspace following their corresponding prompts in a divide-and-conquer manner.\n  3. The system achieves state-of-the-art performance and human-level naturalness on the multi-speaker LibriSpeech dataset for zero-shot text-to-speech synthesis.\n  4. The factorization design enables flexible speech attribute manipulation by allowing different prompts to control different attributes independently.\n  5. The effectiveness of the system is further improved by scaling the model to 1 billion parameters and the training data to 200K hours.\n\nTags: TTS, 压缩, TTS/架构-NAR-Diffusion, TTS/控制-参考语音, 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/语义声学-语义, 压缩/语义声学-声学, 压缩/语义声学-混合\n=== 2409.13216 ===\nPaper 2409.13216 not found\n=== 2509.19883 ===\nPaper 2509.19883 not found\n=== 2509.14052 ===\nPaper 2509.14052 not found\n=== 2508.16332 ===\n# Vevo2: A Unified and Controllable Framework for Speech and Singing Voice Generation\narXiv: 2508.16332\nAuthors: Xueyao Zhang, Junan Zhang, Yuancheng Wang, Chaoren Wang, Yuanzhe Chen, Dongya Jia, Zhuo Chen, Zhizheng Wu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: tts_vc\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We propose two audio tokenizers: a unified music-notation-free prosody tokenizer capable of encoding prosody and melody from speech, singing voice, and even instrumental sounds, and a unified timbre-disentangled content-style tokenizer that models linguistic content, prosody, and style for both speech and singing voice.\n  2. We introduce Vevo2, a unified and controllable framework for both speech and singing voice generation that applies versatile voice synthesis, conversion, and editing tasks by flexibly combining text, prosodic source, and style and timbre references during inference.\n  3. We propose a multi-objective post-training task for controllable voice generation that optimizes both intelligibility and prosody similarity rewards with a GRPO-based algorithm to achieve greater stability and improved overall performance.\n\nTags: TTS, TTS/架构-NAR-Flow, TTS/控制-参考语音, TTS/控制-风格标签, TTS/功能-RL优化, TTS/功能-多说话人", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
